ion above is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = c_{1}x^{2}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} + c_{2}y_{2}(x),\n$$\nwhere $c_{1}$ and $c_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and $a_{n}$ are constants.\nWhich of the following is the form of the second Frobenius solution $y_{2}$, where the constants $b_{n}$ are determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x = 0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $λ^{2} - 4λ + 4 = 0$.*" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186229743 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n$x^{2}y^{″} - 3xy^{′} + 4(x + 1)y = 0$\nThe general solution near $x = 0$ of the differential equation above is given by\n$y = c_{1}x^{2}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} + c_{2}y_{2}(x)$,\nwhere $c_{1}$ and $c_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and $a_{n}$ are constants.\nWhich of the following is the form of the second Frobenius solution $y_{2}$, where the constants $b_{n}$ are determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x = 0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $λ^{2} - 4λ + 4 = 0$.*" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186229744 1074 "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}\n$$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- ${a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1})$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda =0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (3\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nWith $\\lambda =\\frac{2}{3},$ the recurrence relation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{2}{3}+n)(3(\\frac{2}{3}+n)-2)}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3n(n+\\frac{2}{3})}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n(3n+2)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, with ${a}_{0}\\ne 0$ arbitrary, we evaluate the first three coefficients using the given recurrence relation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & \\ne 0, \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{4}{1(3\\cdot 1+2)}\\,{a}_{0}=\\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{4}{2(3\\cdot 2+2)}\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first Frobenius solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & ={x}^{2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} \\\\  & ={x}^{2/3}({a}_{0}+\\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}x+\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{0}{x}^{2}+⋯) \\\\  & ={a}_{0}({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{8/3}+⋯).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186229744 1074 "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- ${a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1})$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda =0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (3\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_