\,\\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $360$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $360{:}$$$\n\\displaystyle 360 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 360 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{360}{12}ft|= 360in\n$$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{360}{12}$ as the division $360 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$0$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$0$$0$Therefore,$360\\,\\textrm{in} = 30\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $30$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd} = 1\\,\\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $30$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $30{:}$$$\n\\displaystyle 30 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 30 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{30}{3}yd|= 30ft; 10yd|= 30ft\n$$Therefore,$30\\,\\textrm{ft} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion,\n\n$$\n360\\,\\textrm{in} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nTo convert inches to yards, we convert inches to feet first, then convert feet to yards." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186232048 1064 "Sometimes we need two conversions to change a measurement into a larger unit.\n\nFor example, to convert inches to yards, we can convert\n\n$$\n\\textrm{inches} \\rightarrow \\textrm{feet} \\rightarrow \\textrm{yards}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $360$ inches into yards. We first convert $360$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $360$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{ft} = 12\\,\\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\,\\textrm{ft} = 1\\,\\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $360$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $360{