 series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}}.\n$$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}} & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\cdot {n}^{2/3}} \\\\  & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{n}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{4}}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=4.$ Since $p>1,$ the series is convergent.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[3]{n}}{\\sqrt{n}} & =5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{1/3}}{{n}^{1/2}} \\\\  & =5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/6}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186233371 1074 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}}.$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}} & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\cdot {n}^{2/3}} \\\\  & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{n}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{4}}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=4.$ Since $p>1,$ the series is convergent.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[3]{n}}{\\sqrt{n}} & =5{\\sum }_