ges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{1+n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{n(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}}{(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186233443 1074 "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{1+n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{n(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}}{(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n