ties $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ and we substitute them into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\operatorname{csc}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta })÷(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\right)÷(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\right)÷(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cancel{(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\o