 recall the properties of convergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}=K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement II is false. The sum of two convergent series is convergent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234121 1071 "For which values of $q$ does the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3q-1}}\n$$ converge?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186234121 1071 "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3q-1}}$ converge?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234122 1071 "For which values of $q$ does the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2q-2}}\n$$ converge?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186234122 1071 "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2q-2}}$ converge?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234123 1071 "For which values of $q$ does the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{4q-1}}\n$$ converge?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186234123 1071 "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{4q-1}}$ converge?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234124 1071 "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}\n$$\nII. - $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[9]{{n}^{2}}}\n$$\nIII. - $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}{2{n}^{2}}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186234124 1071 "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[9]{{n}^{2}}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}{2{n}^{2}}$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234124 1074 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}.\n$$ Rewriting this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\cdot {n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[9]{{n}^{2}}}=8\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2/9}},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{2}{9}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}{2{n}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3/2}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186234124 1074 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}.$ Rewriting this gives\n\n$$