wing is true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=100}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}?$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234386 1074 "Removing the first $k-1$ terms from a convergent series results in a convergent series.\n\nMore precisely, assume that $k\\ge 1$ is an integer. Then, we have the following:\n\n*If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ is convergent, then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ is also convergent.*\n\nSo, since $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ is convergent, we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=100}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ is also convergent." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186234386 1074 "Removing the first $k-1$ terms from a convergent series results in a convergent series.\n\nMore precisely, assume that $k\\ge 1$ is an integer. Then, we have the following:\n\n*If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.*\n\nSo, since ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, we have that ${\\sum }_{n=100}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234427 1071 "Which of the following series converge?\n\nI. - $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}\n$$\nII. - $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}}\n$$\nIII. - $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186234427 1071 "Which of the following series converge?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234427 1074 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}.\n$$ Rewriting this gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/4}},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186234427 1074 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}.$ Rewriting this gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/4}},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234428 1071 "Which of the following seri